Context Window
•	The maximum number of tokens (chunks of text) the model can keep in memory at once.
•	Larger context windows mean the model can handle longer conversations, documents, or code without “forgetting” earlier parts.
•	Example: A 1M context window (Claude Sonnet 4.5) can process an entire book or huge codebase in one go, while 400K (GPT 5 Nano) is smaller but still very large.
Input Tokens
•	Tokens you send to the model (your prompt, instructions, or uploaded text).
•	Pricing is usually per million input tokens.
•	Example: If you paste a 10,000 word document, those words are converted into tokens and billed as input.
Output Tokens
•	Tokens the model generates in response (its answer, completion, or continuation).
•	Pricing is per million output tokens.
•	Example: If the model writes a 5,000 word essay back, those words are billed as output.
Putting It Together
•	Context window = memory capacity (how much text the model can consider at once).
•	Input tokens = what you pay for sending text in.
•	Output tokens = what you pay for the model’s generated text out.
•  Claude Sonnet 4.5: Huge memory (1M tokens), but expensive per input/output.
•	GPT 5 Nano: Smaller memory (400K), but very cheap per token.
•	Qwen3 235B: Mid range memory (262K), with balanced pricing.

